    # French. Machine translated, NOT reviewed by a native speaker - see the header of
    # Tlc.Localization.psm1. Safe for what it covers: field labels, buttons and hints.
    # Nothing that reaches a bundle is translated.
    #
    # Product names stay in English on purpose. "Teams admin center", "Teams Rooms" and
    # "PowerShell" are what the controls in those products actually say, so translating them
    # would send an engineer looking for something that does not exist under that name.
    #
    # One word per concept: "journal"/"journaux" for log, "trace" for trace, everywhere.
    #
    # Every apostrophe is DOUBLED. These are single-quoted PowerShell strings, so a lone
    # apostrophe in "l'outil" ends the string and the file stops parsing - which in French
    # is not an edge case but most of the language.
    #
    # THIS FILE MUST BE SAVED AS UTF-8 WITH A BOM. Without one, Import-PowerShellDataFile on
    # Windows PowerShell 5.1 reads the bytes as the ANSI codepage and every accent becomes
    # mojibake, while PowerShell 7 reads it correctly either way.

